          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.721,
            0.304,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A step further"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.757,
            0.735,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In everyday life, particularly for the goods we commonly use, we are more interested in the amount of matter in an object (its mass), rather than the force applied by the Earth upon it (its weight). However, though while the units of mass are used, instead of the term mass, the term weight is typically used. For example, it is said that the weight of the wheat bag is 10 kg. But in scientific use, this is not correct and it is important to use the correct terms with their correct units, even if every day language is more casual."
            }
          ]
        },
